" (https://efile.fara.gov/docs/7261-Amendment-20250428-3.pdf)\nciteturn186view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 3; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://efile.fara.gov/docs/7261-Amendment-20250428-3.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 67\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nU.S.C. Title 2 - THE CONGRESS (https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2010-title2/html/USCODE-2010-title2-chap26-sec1603.htm)\nciteturn186view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn184search1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 104\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicrosoft Word - TCPL AIF 2009 _v6_.doc (https://www.tcenergy.com/siteassets/pdfs/investors/shareholders/historical-information/tcpl-regulatory-filings/2009/tcpl-2009-annual-information-form.pdf)\nciteturn186view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 87; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn182view0\",\"lineno\":124}); Total lines: 4648\nL81@P2: reference into this AIF as stated below. The MD&A can be found on SEDAR at www.sedar.com under TCPL's profile.\nL82@P2: The Accounting Standards Board (\"AcSB\") of the Canadian Institute of Chartered Accountants has announced that Canadian\nL83@P2: publicly accountable enterprises are required to adopt International Financial Reporting Standards (\"IFRS\"), as issued by the\nL84@P2: International Accounting Standards Board, effective January 1, 2011. Effective January 1, 2011, TCPL will begin reporting under\nL85@P2: IFRS. TCPL’s conversion plan includes obtaining skilled people, providing education and training, analyzing the impact on\nL86@P2: TCPL of key differences between Canadian GAAP and IFRS, and developing and executing a phased approach to conversion and\nL87@P2: implementation. For more information on TCPL’s conversion project, see TCPL’s MD&A under \"Accounting Changes –\nL88@P2: International Financial Reporting Standards\".\nL89@P2: Information relating to metric conversion can be found at Schedule \"A\" to this AIF. Terms defined throughout this AIF are listed\nL90@P2: in the Glossary found at the end of this AIF.\nL91@P2: FORWARD LOOKING INFORMATION\nL92@P2: This AIF, the documents incorporated by reference into this AIF, and other reports and filings made with the securities regulatory\nL93@P2: authorities may contain certain information that is forward looking and is subject to important risks and uncertainties. The words\nL94@P2: \"anticipate\", \"expect\", \"believe\", \"may\", \"should\", \"estimate\", \"project\", \"outlook\", \"forecast\" or other similar words are used to\nL95@P2: identify such forward looking information. Forward-looking statements in this document are intended to provide securityholders\nL96@P2: and potential investors with information regarding TCPL and its subsidiaries, including management’s assessment of TCPL’s and\nL97@P2: its subsidiaries’ future financial and operational plans and outlook. Forward-looking statements in this document may include,\nL98@P2: among others, statements regarding the anticipated business prospects and financial performance of TCPL and its subsidiaries,\nL99@P2: expectations or projections about the future, strategies and goals for growth and expansion, expected and future cash flows, costs,\nL100@P2: schedules, operating and financial results and expected impact of future commitments and contingent liabilities. All forward\nL101@P2: looking statements reflect TCPL's beliefs and assumptions based on information available at the time the statements were made.\nL102@P2: Actual results or events may differ from those predicted in these forward-looking statements. Factors that could cause actual\nL103@P2: results or events to differ materially from current expectations include, among others, the ability of TCPL to successfully\nL104@P2: implement its strategic initiatives and whether such strategic initiatives will yield the expected benefits, the operating performance\nL105@P2: of the Company's pipeline and energy assets, the availability and price of energy commodities, capacity payments, regulatory\nL106@P2: processes and decisions, changes in environmental and other laws and regulations, competitive factors in the pipeline and energy\nL107@P2: sectors, construction and completion of capital projects, labour, equipment and material costs, access to capital markets, interest\nL108@P2: and currency exchange rates, technological developments and economic conditions in North America. By its nature, forward\nL109@P2: looking information is subject to various risks and uncertainties, including those material risks discussed in this AIF under \"Risk\nL110@P2: Factors\", which could cause TCPL's actual results and experience to differ materially from the anticipated results or expectations\nL111@P2: expressed. Additional information on these and other factors is available in the reports filed by TCPL with Canadian securities\nL112@P2: regulators and with the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (\"SEC\"). Readers are cautioned to not place undue reliance on\nL113@P2: this forward looking information, which is given as of the date it is expressed in this AIF or otherwise, and to not use future\u0002oriented information or financial outlooks for anything other than their intended purpose. TCPL undertakes no obligation to\nL114@P2: update publicly or revise any forward-looking information, whether as a result of new information, future events or otherwise,\nL115@P2-3: except as required by law. TRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED 1\nL116@P3: TRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED\nL117@P3: Corporate Structure\nL118@P3: TCPL’s head office and registered office are located at 450 - 1st Street S.W., Calgary, Alberta, T2P 5H1.\nL119@P3: TCPL is a Canadian public company. Significant dates and events are set forth below.\nL120@P3: Date Event\nL121@P3: March 21, 1951 Incorporated by Special Act of Parliament as Trans-Canada Pipe Lines Limited.\nL122@P3: April 19, 1972\nL123@P3: Continued under the Canada Corporations Act by Letters Patent, which included the alteration of its capital and change\nL124@P3: of name to TransCanada PipeLines Limited.\nL125@P3: June 1, 1979 Continued under the Canada Business Corporations Act.\nL126@P3: July 2, 1998 Certificate of Arrangement issued in connection with the Plan of Arrangement with NOVA Corporation (\"NOVA\")\nL127@P3: under which the companies merged and then split off the commodity chemicals business carried on by NOVA into a\nL128@P3: separate public company.\nL129@P3: January 1, 1999 Certificate of Amalgamation issued reflecting TCPL’s vertical short form amalgamation with a wholly owned\nL130@P3: subsidiary, Alberta Natural Gas Company Ltd.\nL131@P3: January 1, 2000 Certificate of Amalgamation issued reflecting TCPL’s vertical short form amalgamation with a wholly owned\nL132@P3: subsidiary, NOVA Gas International Ltd.\nL133@P3: May 4, 2001 Restated TransCanada PipeLines Limited Articles of Incorporation filed.\nL134@P3: June 20, 2002 Restated TransCanada PipeLines Limited By-Laws filed.\nL135@P3: May 15, 2003 Certificate of Arrangement issued in connection with the plan of arrangement with TransCanada. TransCanada was\nL136@P3: incorporated pursuant to the provisions of the Canada Business Corporations Act on February 25, 2003. The\nL137@P3: arrangement was approved by TCPL common shareholders on April 25, 2003 and following court approval, Articles of\nL138@P3: Arrangement were filed making the arrangement effective May 15, 2003. The common shareholders of TCPL\nL139@P3: exchanged each of their TCPL common shares for one common share of TransCanada. The debt securities and preferred\nL140@P3: shares of TCPL remained obligations and securities of TCPL. TCPL continues to hold the assets it held prior to the\nL141@P3: arrangement and continues to carry on business as the principal operating subsidiary of the TransCanada group of\nL142@P3: entities.\nL143@P3: Intercorporate Relationships\nL144@P3: The following diagram presents the name and jurisdiction of incorporation, continuance or formation of TCPL’s principal\nL145@P3: subsidiaries as at December 31, 2009. Each of these subsidiaries has total assets that exceeded 10% of the total consolidated\nL146@P3: assets of TransCanada or revenues that exceeded 10% of the total consolidated revenues of TransCanada as at and for the year\nL147@P3: ended December 31, 2009. TCPL owns, directly or indirectly, 100 per cent of the voting shares of each of these subsidiaries.\nL148@P3: TransCanada\nL149@P3: Corporation\nL150@P3: Canada\nL151@P3: TransCanada\nL152@P3: PipeLines Limited\nL153@P3: Canada\nL154@P3: TransCanada PipeLine\nL155@P3: USA Ltd.\nL156@P3: Nevada\nL157@P3: TransCanada Power\nL158@P3: Marketing Ltd.\nL159@P3: Delaware\nL160@P3: NOVA Gas\nL161@P3: Transmission Ltd.\nL162@P3: Alberta\nL163@P3: 701671 Alberta Ltd.\nL164@P3: Alberta\nL165@P3: TransCanada Hungary\nL166@P3: Liquidity Management LLC\nL167@P3: Hungary\nL168@P3: Global Infrastructure\nL169@P3: Holdings Ltd.\nL170@P3: Bermuda\nL171@P3: TransCanada Energy Ltd.\nL172@P3: Canada\nL173@P3: TransCanada Energy\nL174@P3: Investments Ltd.\nL175@P3: Canada\nL176@P3: TransCanada American\nL177@P3: Investments Ltd.\nL178@P3: Delaware\nL179@P3: TransCanada\nL180@P3: Corporation\nL181@P3: Canada\nL182@P3: TransCanada\nL183@P3: PipeLines Limited\nL184@P3: Canada\nL185@P3: TransCanada PipeLine\nL186@P3: USA Ltd.\nL187@P3: Nevada\nL188@P3: TransCanada Power\nL189@P3: Marketing Ltd.\nL190@P3: Delaware\nL191@P3: NOVA Gas\nL192@P3: Transmission Ltd.\nL193@P3: Alberta\nL194@P3: 701671 Alberta Ltd.\nL195@P3: Alberta\nL196@P3: TransCanada Hungary\nL197@P3: Liquidity Management LLC\nL198@P3: Hungary\nL199@P3: Global Infrastructure\nL200@P3: Holdings Ltd.\nL201@P3: Bermuda\nL202@P3: TransCanada Energy Ltd.\nL203@P3: Canada\nL204@P3: TransCanada Energy\nL205@P3: Investments Ltd.\nL206@P3: Canada\nL207@P3: TransCanada American\nL208@P3: Investments Ltd.\nL209@P3-4: DelawareTRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED 2\nL210@P4: The above diagram does not include all of the subsidiaries of TCPL. The assets and revenues of excluded subsidiaries in the\nL211@P4: aggregate did not exceed 20% of the total consolidated assets or total consolidated revenues of TCPL as at and for the year ended\nL212@P4: December 31, 2009.\nL213@P4: GENERAL DEVELOPMENT OF THE BUSINESS\nL214@P4: The general development of TCPL's business during the last three financial years, and the significant acquisitions, dispositions,\nL215@P4: events or conditions which have had an influence on that development, are described below.\nL216@P4: TCPL's reportable business segments are Pipelines and Energy. Pipelines are principally comprised of the Company's pipelines in\nL217@P4: Canada, the U.S. and Mexico and its regulated natural gas storage operations in the U.S. Energy includes the Company's power\nL218@P4: operations and the non-regulated natural gas storage business.\nL219@P4: Developments in the Pipelines Business\nL220@P4: TCPL's strategy in Pipelines is focused on both growing its North American natural gas and crude oil transmission network and\nL221@P4: maximizing the long-term value of its existing pipeline assets. Summarized below are significant developments that have\nL222@P4: occurred in TCPL's Pipelines business over the last three years.\nL223@P4: 2010\nL224@P4: Pipeline Developments\nL225@P4: • January 29, 2010. TCPL announced that the proposed Alaska pipeline project (the \"Alaska Pipeline Project\") filed\nL226@P4: its plan with the United States Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (\"FERC\") to obtain approval to conduct an\nL227@P4: open season. If approval is granted, an open season offering is expected to be provided to potential shippers at the\nL228@P4: end of April 2010 for their assessment until July 2010. The Alaska Pipeline Project is a 4.5 billion cubic feet per day\nL229@P4: (\"Bcf/d\") natural gas pipeline that would extend 2,737 kilometres (\"km\") (1,700 miles) from a new natural gas\nL230@P4: treatment plant at Prudhoe Bay, Alaska to Alberta.\nL231@P4: Regulatory Matters\nL232@P4: • February 19, 2010. TCPL filed an application with the National Energy Board (\"NEB\") for approvals to construct\nL233@P4: and operate the proposed Horn River pipeline project (\"Horn River Project\"), a 158 km (98 mile) pipeline and related\nL234@P4: facilities to connect new shale gas supply in the Horn River basin north of Fort Nelson, B.C., to TCPL’s natural gas\nL235@P4: transmission system in the province of Alberta (the \"Alberta System\"). The Horn River Project will consist of\nL236@P4: approximately 74 km of new pipelines and the purchase and use of an existing pipeline in the Horn River area and\nL237@P4: will transport sweet natural gas to a tie in point on the Alberta System. The project is expected to cost approximately\nL238@P4: $307 million.\nL239@P4: 2009\nL240@P4: Pipeline Developments\nL241@P4: • February 26, 2009. TCPL announced the successful completion of a binding open season, securing support for firm\nL242@P4: transportation contracts of 378 million cubic feet per day (\"MMcf/d\") for the Horn River Project. Total contractual\nL243@P4: commitments for the Horn River Project increased to 503 MMcf/d by 2014 as a result of newly contracted volumes\nL244@P4: from a recently announced natural gas processing facility that will be located in the Horn River area.\nL245@P4: • May 7, 2009. TCPL announced that it was the successful bidder on a contract to build, own and operate a US$320\nL246@P4: million pipeline in Mexico, which is supported by a twenty-five year contract for its entire capacity with Comisión\nL247@P4: Federal de Electridad, Mexico’s state-owned electric power company. The proposed pipeline, known as the\nL248@P4: Guadalajara Pipeline, is an approximately 305 km (190 mile) pipeline capable of transporting 500 MMcf/d of natural\nL249@P4: gas, and is proposed to extend from a liquefied natural gas terminal under construction near Manzanillo on Mexico’s\nL250@P4: Pacific Coast to Guadalajara, the second largest city in Mexico. Regulatory approvals were received in December\nL251@P4: 2009 and construction is under way with an expected in-service date of first quarter 2011.\nL252@P4: • June 11, 2009. TCPL reached an agreement with ExxonMobil Corporation to jointly advance the Alaska Pipeline\nL253@P4: Project. A joint project team is developing the engineering, environmental, aboriginal relations and commercial\nL254@P4-5: work. TRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED 3\nL255@P5: • July 1, 2009. TCPL completed the sale of North Baja Pipeline, LLC (\"North Baja\") to its affiliate TC PipeLines, LP.\nL256@P5: As part of the transaction, TCPL agreed to amend its incentive distribution rights with TC PipeLines, LP. Under the\nL257@P5: amendment, TCPL received additional common units in exchange for a resetting of its incentive distribution rights at\nL258@P5: a lower percentage which escalates with increases in TC PipeLines, LP distributions. The aggregate consideration\nL259@P5: received from the partnership included a combination of cash and common units totaling approximately US$395\nL260@P5: million. With the close of the transaction, TCPL’s ownership of the partnership increased to 42.6 per cent. TCPL\nL261@P5: continued to operate North Baja following the transfer of ownership. The system is a 129 km (80 mile) natural gas\nL262@P5: pipeline that extends from southwest Arizona to a point on the California/Mexico border and connects with a natural\n"
